(window.LADDER_CARDS["37a"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["37a"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"37a1.1","q":"What does p(x, t) measure, what sign has it where the air is thinned, and why is “air moving towards you” not what the ear meets?","a":"The pressure at place x and time t minus the steady pressure of still air; it is negative where the air is thinned; the air only moves back and forth along the way the sound goes, and what the ear meets is the pressure, not the motion","w":["The whole pressure of the air, about 101 000 newtons a square metre; it is 0 where the air is thinned; the ear meets a wind","The pressure minus the steady pressure; it is positive where the air is thinned; the ear meets air that has travelled from the source","How far the air has moved; it has no sign; the ear meets the motion and not the pressure"],"why":"Speech swings p by about two parts in ten million of the steady pressure."},
 {"u":"37a2.1","q":"What does the air-wave technique conclude, on which two laws held in trust, and when a clap crosses a beach what travels and what stays?","a":"For small pushes the air in a pipe and its sound pressure obey the wave equation with c = √(K/ρ); it stands on the stiffness law p = −K u_x and the law of motion ρ u_tt = −p_x; the squeeze, the shape, travels, and the air at each place only swings about its rest","w":["The air obeys the heat equation with c = K/ρ; it stands on the stiffness law alone; the air itself travels from the clap to the ear","The air obeys the wave equation with c = √(K/ρ); it stands on the stiffness law and the law of motion; the air travels and the squeeze stays","The air obeys the wave equation with c = √(ρ/K); it stands on two measured speeds; nothing travels"],"why":"Everything 33c2 proved about the string holds for the air in a pipe."},
 {"u":"37a3.1","q":"What does the medium-speed technique say, and how can sound run faster in water, which is some 830 times as heavy as air?","a":"Every sound in one still medium runs at that medium’s one speed, whatever its shape, loudness or pitch; water is heavier but stiffer by far more, and c = √(K/ρ), so about 1480 m/s against 343","w":["Louder and higher sounds run faster; water is faster because it is heavier","Every sound in one medium runs at one speed; water is faster because heavy media always carry sound faster","Every sound runs at 343 m/s in every medium; in water it only seems faster"],"why":"A sound does not choose its speed; the medium sets it."},
 {"u":"37a4.1","q":"What does the wavelength technique say, and how long is the wave of a bat’s call at 40 000 Hz in air, set against a moth?","a":"Frequency times wavelength is the speed, f λ = c; λ = 343/40 000, about 8.6 mm, a little shorter than a moth","w":["Frequency plus wavelength is the speed; λ is about 8.6 m, far longer than a moth","f λ = c; λ = 40 000/343, about 117 m, far longer than a moth","Wavelength is frequency over speed; λ is about 0.86 mm, a tenth of that"],"why":"The cosine repeats along x when x grows by c/f."},
 {"u":"37a5.1","q":"“A shout reaches you sooner than a whisper.” What is wrong, and what settles it?","a":"Loudness does not change the speed: by the medium-speed technique every sound in the same air runs at the one speed c","w":["Nothing is wrong: louder sounds push the air faster","The whisper is sooner, since quiet sounds lose less on the way; the spreading technique","The shout is sooner only if it is higher; the wavelength technique"],"why":"c = √(K/ρ) has no loudness in it."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["37b"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["37b"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"37b1.1","q":"What does the repeat-rate technique say of a sound that repeats every T, and what are the repeat and the pitch of turnings at 300 Hz and 450 Hz sounded together?","a":"It is a sum of turnings at the whole multiples of 1/T and nothing else; 300 and 450 are both multiples of 150, so the sum repeats every 1/150 of a second and its pitch is 150 Hz, though nothing at 150 Hz is in it","w":["It is one turning at 1/T; the sum repeats every 1/300 s, pitch 300 Hz, the lower turning","It is a sum of turnings at the multiples of 1/T; the sum repeats every 1/750 s, pitch 750 Hz, the two added","It is a sum of turnings at the multiples of 1/T; the sum never repeats, so it has no pitch"],"why":"The technique does not need the turning at 1/T itself to be there."},
 {"u":"37b2.1","q":"What is the hearing window and why is it laid down and not proved, how many octaves does the ten-octave technique count, and how many would 40 Hz to 15 000 Hz hold?","a":"The frequencies from about 20 Hz to about 20 000 Hz, a fact by cases drawn from many listeners with a spread; log₂ 1000, about 9.97 octaves; from 40 to 15 000 it is log₂ 375, about 8.55","w":["From 20 Hz to 20 000 Hz, proved from the wave equation; 1000 octaves; 375 octaves","From about 20 Hz to about 20 000 Hz, measured from listeners; log₁₀ 1000 = 3 octaves; about 2.6","From about 20 Hz to about 20 000 Hz, measured from listeners; about 9.97 octaves; about 9.47, half an octave less"],"why":"An octave is a doubling, so count the doublings."},
 {"u":"37b3.1","q":"What does the pitch-line technique say, and how many cents is the pure major third 5/4, against the four-semitone third of equal tuning?","a":"The distance between two notes is log₂ of their ratio, and stacked intervals add; 1200 · log₂ 1.25 is about 386 cents, about 14 cents short of the 400 of equal tuning","w":["The distance is the difference of the frequencies; 5/4 is 125 cents, far below 400","The distance is log₂ of the ratio; 5/4 is about 414 cents, 14 above the equal third","The distance is log₂ of the ratio; 5/4 is exactly 400 cents, the same as the equal third"],"why":"h(f) = log₂(f/440), with 1200 cents to the octave."},
 {"u":"37b4.1","q":"A note has turnings at 220, 440 and 660 Hz, loudest at 440. What is its pitch, and which reading of “pitch” should be checked against a tuner’s 220 Hz?","a":"220 Hz: the pitch is how often the sound repeats, 1/T, not its loudest turning","w":["440 Hz, the loudest turning; the tuner is wrong","660 Hz, the highest turning","It has three pitches at once"],"why":"The repeat-rate technique: the pitch need not be the loudest turning, or even present."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["37c"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["37c"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"37c1.1","q":"What does the adding-power technique say, why must the frequencies differ, and how many times one note’s intensity do two equal 440 Hz notes in step carry?","a":"Sounds at different frequencies arriving together have intensities that add, since turnings at different frequencies are at right angles and the cross term averages to 0; at one frequency in step the pressures add, so twice the pressure and four times the intensity","w":["Intensities always add; two equal notes in step carry twice the intensity","Intensities add at different frequencies because the ear separates them; two equal notes in step carry the same intensity as one","Pressures always add; two notes at different frequencies carry four times the intensity"],"why":"I = ⟨p²⟩/(ρc), and ⟨(p + q)²⟩ has the cross term 2⟨pq⟩."},
 {"u":"37c2.1","q":"What does the pressure-level technique say, why twenty times a log, and how many decibels separate two sounds whose pressures are ten to one?","a":"The level is 20 log₁₀(p_rms/p_ref) with p_ref = 20 micropascals, agreeing with 10 log₁₀(I/I₀) to a small fraction of a decibel; intensity is the square of pressure, and the log of a square is twice the log; ten times the pressure is 20 dB","w":["The level is 10 log₁₀(p_rms/p_ref); ten times the pressure is 10 dB","The level is 20 log₁₀(p_rms/p_ref) because a microphone has two sides; ten times the pressure is 100 dB","The level is 20 · p_rms/p_ref; ten times the pressure is 200 dB"],"why":"Doubling the pressure adds 6 dB; doubling the power adds 3 dB."},
 {"u":"37c3.1","q":"What does the spreading technique say, and about how loud at 80 m in open air is a siren that is 100 dB at 10 m?","a":"From a small source in open air the level falls 6 dB each time the distance doubles; 80 m is three doublings of 10 m, so about 82 dB","w":["The level halves each time the distance doubles; about 12.5 dB","The level falls 6 dB each time the distance doubles; 80 m is eight times as far, so about 52 dB","The level falls 3 dB for each doubling; about 91 dB"],"why":"I(r) = W/(4πr²), and 10 log₁₀ ¼ is about −6."},
 {"u":"37c4.1","q":"In which three ways does the ear fail to keep a sound’s power as it is, and why are these laid down and not proved?","a":"It weighs the power by frequency, keenest from about 2000 to 5000 Hz; it drops a quieter sound that a louder neighbour in frequency hides; and past about 120 to 130 dB it is hurt and deafened; they are measured from many listeners, facts by cases with their rates","w":["It adds pressures in place of powers; it hears only whole multiples; it drops the turns; all three are proved from the wave equation","It weighs by frequency; it drops what a louder neighbour hides; it is deafened past a point; they are laid down because no one has measured them","It hears nothing below 2000 Hz; it hears loud and quiet alike; it cannot be harmed; they are axioms"],"why":"A law with a known exception rate is still a law."},
 {"u":"37c5.1","q":"Two coffee grinders, 70 dB each, run together. What level do they make, and what slip gives 140 dB?","a":"About 73 dB: their powers add, and doubling the power adds 3 dB; the slip is adding decibels, which are logs","w":["140 dB: levels add","70 dB: a second equal sound adds nothing","76 dB: two sounds always double the pressure"],"why":"Two different machines are not in step, so the adding-power technique applies."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["37d"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["37d"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"37d1.1","q":"What are a note’s recipe and its quality, what does the recipe technique say, and which two techniques of rung 32 does its proof spend?","a":"The recipe is the list of amounts and turns (rₙ, φₙ) of its turnings, and the quality the amounts as shares of the whole; two notes that repeat every T are the same sound exactly when their recipes agree; the proof spends 32b3, energy is kept, and 32c2, where the sum returns the function","w":["The recipe is the pitch and the level; the quality is the loudest turning; notes with one pitch and level are the same sound; no technique is spent","The recipe is the list of amounts and turns; the quality is the list of turns; two notes are the same exactly when their qualities agree; it spends 32a1 alone","The recipe is the list of amounts; the quality is the pitch; recipes never agree for two instruments; it spends the wave equation"],"why":"A flute and a clarinet: same pitch, same level, different recipes."},
 {"u":"37d2.1","q":"What does the crest-meeting technique say, and is a place 2.25 m from one speaker and 2.0 m from the other loud or quiet when both play 686 Hz in step?","a":"Two equal turnings in step add to the most where the paths differ by a whole number of wavelengths and to nothing where they differ by a whole number and a half; at 686 Hz λ = 0.5 m, and 0.25 m is half a wavelength: quiet","w":["They add to twice the loudness everywhere; the place is loud","They add to the most at whole wavelengths and to nothing at halves; λ = 0.25 m, so the paths differ by one wavelength: loud","They cancel where the paths differ by a whole number of wavelengths; λ = 0.5 m, so the place is loud"],"why":"The size is 2|cos(π(d₁ − d₂)/λ)|."},
 {"u":"37d3.1","q":"How many throbs a second do 220 Hz and 224 Hz make, what does the ear hear when the gap is 150 Hz near 500 Hz, and why is that laid down?","a":"Four; two notes, each clean, since the gap is past the hundred or so hertz within which it hears one rough note; where the ear passes from a throb to roughness to two notes is measured from listeners, a fact by cases with its rate","w":["Four; one note that throbs 150 times a second; it is proved from the sum formulas","444; one rough note; it is laid down because the formulas fail","Two; two notes, each clean; it is proved from the wave equation"],"why":"The swell 2|cos(π(f₁ − f₂)t)| peaks |f₁ − f₂| times a second."},
 {"u":"37d4.1","q":"What does the turn-free technique say, why is there no φₙ in the intensity at the nth frequency, and what in the footing is laid down, for which sounds?","a":"Changing the turns of a note’s turnings changes neither its pitch nor its intensity nor the intensity at any one frequency; that intensity is rₙ²/(2ρc), built from the amount alone; laid down, as measured: for steady notes the ear hears the amounts and is nearly deaf to the turns","w":["Changing the turns changes the pitch and not the intensity; φₙ cancels only on average; it is laid down for every sound","Changing the turns changes nothing about the wave; the intensity is rₙφₙ; nothing is laid down","Changing the turns changes neither pitch nor intensity; the intensity is rₙ²/(2ρc); it is proved that no ear can ever hear a turn, for clicks as for notes"],"why":"cos t + cos 3t and cos t − cos 3t look different and have the same amounts."},
 {"u":"37d5.1","q":"Two notes at 440 and 444 Hz throb four times a second, and someone says a 4 Hz note is in the room. What is there?","a":"No turning at 4 Hz: the two turnings at 440 and 444 Hz, whose sum swells and fades in loudness four times a second","w":["A 4 Hz note too low to hear","A single note at 442 Hz and a single note at 4 Hz","Nothing: the two notes cancel"],"why":"Beats are a swell in loudness, not a turning at |f₁ − f₂|."});
